(g) The Sheriff or his designee shall select and designate the required automated reporting system.
(Ord. of 8-1-2013(6), § 6)

392:
This article is established to:
(1) Provide for the public health, public safety, and general welfare of the citizens of the unincorporated areas of Walker County;
(2) Preserve, restore, and enhance the quality of the environment of Walker County;
(3) Set policies for the protection of particularly sensitive environments;
(4) Regulate certain materials, facilities, and activities which if unregulated would have the potential to impair or damage the public health, public safety, general welfare, and quality of the environment in and for Walker County and the citizens thereof;
(5) Grant exemptions and variances for just cause and where the general purposes of the article would not be impaired;
(6) Provide for investigation and enforcement; and
(7) Confer such powers and duties as are necessary and useful to attain the objectives of this section.
(Code 2005, § 26-31; Ord. of 9-3-1991, art. I, § 1; Ord. of 2-23-2012, § 26-31)

406:
(a) Except as otherwise provided under this article, no person shall, and it shall be unlawful and a violation of this section, in an unincorporated area of the County, without first obtaining and possessing a special use permit from the Board of Commissioners:
(1) Engage in the disposal of any:
a. Biomedical waste;
b. Contaminant;
c. Designated hazardous waste;
d. Hazardous chemical substance or mixture;
e. Hazardous constituent;
f. Hazardous substance;
g. Hazardous waste;
h. Municipal solid waste;
i. Oil;
j. Pesticide;
k. Solid waste; or
l. Special solid waste; or
(2) Construct, install, operate or substantially alter any:
a. Composting facility;
b. Land disposal facility;
c. Landfill;
d. Municipal solid waste disposal facility;
e. Municipal solid waste landfill; or
f. Private sanitary landfill.
(b) An application for a special use permit shall be submitted to the Planning Commission in such manner and on such forms as the Board of Commissioners or Planning Commission may prescribe and shall be accompanied by an application fee per the fee schedule.
(c) A special use permit shall be issued to an applicant on evidence, satisfactory to the Board of Commissioners, of compliance with this article and any standards, requirements, orders, rules and regulations effective pursuant to this article.
(Code 2005, § 26-81; Ord. of 9-3-1991, art. IV, § 1)

407:
(a) For the purpose of this section, the term "applicant" means any person or individual applying for a special use permit, and, for the purpose of supplying information as part of the application, means any individual or person having a legal or equitable interest of at least 5 percent in the person actually applying for the permit or against the interest of the individual applying for the permit.
(b) The application must sufficiently show the following:
(1) That the applicant, if a corporation, is in good standing with the Secretary of State;
(2) The names and addresses of all persons having a legal or equitable ownership interest of at least 1 percent in the business proposing the use;
(3) The names and addresses of all directors and officers of the applicant if a corporation;
(4) Whether the applicant or any owner, director or officer, or any other person that the applicant has ever been associated with, has ever been convicted of a felony or misdemeanor, whether or not relating to environmental offenses;
(5) Whether the applicant or any owner, director, or officer, or any other person that the applicant has ever been associated with, has ever been charged, cited or had suit brought against him for violation of any law, rule, regulation or order relating to protection of the environment;
(6) The name of the registered agent for service of process and notices, if the applicant is a corporation;
(7) That the applicant, if doing business under a name other than its legal name, has filed its trade name in the County trade name registry;
(8) All claims, litigation and threatened litigation at the time of the application, whether or not in the State;
(9) Certified copies of deeds to real estate in which the applicant has a legal or equitable interest, whether or not in the State, of deeds showing ownership rights of other persons in such real estate, and of instruments showing liens, security interests and other encumbrances;
(10) Current financial statement of the applicant, showing assets, liabilities, income expenditures and a budget for the current year;
(11) Copies of the applicant's financial statements and all State and Federal income tax returns for the 10 years next preceding the application, and budgets for the years next preceding and next following the year of application, if these have been prepared;
(12) All facilities that the applicant owns, operates, or uses, whether or not inside the State, similar in character to the use or facility for which the application has been made;
(13) Whether the applicant, or any owner, officer or director thereof, or any other person that the applicant has ever been associated with, has ever been denied a permit for a use or facility, or proposed use or facility similar in character to the use or facility for which the application has been made;
(14) That the applicant has obtained all necessary business licenses;
(15) That the applicant has made all necessary applications for permits, licenses and approvals from State and Federal environmental regulatory agencies for the use or facility for which the application was made, and that none has been denied which would preclude the use or facility, and copies of the applications and official action shall be attached;
(16) A plan, drawn to scale, showing the boundaries and dimensions of the property; names of the owners of abutting properties; proposed location of the various uses of the property; location and character of all proposed buildings and structures; the type and location of drainage, sewage, water and other utilities at the premises; and the location of proposed roads and driveways and parking facilities;
(17) Adequate space for off-street parking will be provided;
(18) A 500-yard setback buffer distance will be maintained between the nearest building line of any residential dwelling in the vicinity of the property in question and any area of the property where activity requiring permitting is to be carried on;
(19) A 350-yard setback buffer distance will be maintained between the nearest building line of any nonresidential building structure used for human occupancy (for example, retail stores, industrial buildings, and commercial offices) in the vicinity of the property and any area of the property where activity requiring permitting is to be carried on;
(20) A 100-yard setback buffer distance will be maintained between the entire outer boundary of the property and any area of the property where activity requiring permitting is to be carried on;
(21) That such use will not be within a 2-mile radius of the most significant groundwater recharge areas shown on the comprehensive land use plan;
(22) That the use shall be consistent with the comprehensive land use plan for the area;
(23) That adequate public utilities, infrastructure and facilities to service all operations of the applicant exist;
(24) Considering the existing traffic conditions at and near the property and the traffic conditions that will result from the proposed use of the property, adequate traffic routes and entrances will be established; road widths on roads within and leading to the property will be adequate; pavement conditions (material, thickness, age, etc.) will be adequate; and adequate and safe road intersections will be established;
(25) The lot size is sufficient in relation to the proposed use;
(26) That screening will, within 60 days of the granting of a license, be placed within 10 feet of the entire exterior boundary of the tract in question, suitable to, as much as is reasonably practical, alleviate surrounding properties from the burden of noise and unsightly materials and structures. The screening must be no less than 6 feet high, and except where there are driveways or it would otherwise be impractical, the screening must be no less than 3 feet deep; and
(27) Full compliance with the financial responsibility requirements as stated in division 5 of this article.
(c) The Board of Commissioners or Planning Commission may require such other plans, data, specifications, engineering reports, designs and other information as he or it deems necessary, at the cost of the applicant, to make a determination of compliance with this article and the standards, requirements, or rules and regulations promulgated pursuant to this article.
(Code 2005, § 26-82; Ord. of 9-3-1991, art. IV, § 2)

412:
(a) The owner or operator of any activity or facility requiring a special use permit, including an activity or facility granted interim status, shall pay, as a condition of that permit, fees, in addition to the application fee, sufficient to cover the reasonable costs and expenses of:
(1) Reviewing and acting upon any application for a permit or renewal thereof;
(2) Implementing and enforcing the terms of the permit;
(3) Notices required by this article;
(4) Monitoring, investigation and enforcement of activities and facilities requiring permits;
(5) Preparing generally applicable regulations and guidance;
(6) Modeling, analyses and demonstrations; and
(7) All other expenses of the Board of Commissioners in connection with this article and related Federal, State and local law, rules, regulations and orders.
(b) The fee in an amount to be determined by the Board of Commissioners from time to time per ton or volume equivalent of substances received by a site or facility for disposal under this article.
(c) All fees collected under this section shall be paid into a substance disposal trust fund and shall be expended only in connection with the purposes of this article.
(Code 2005, § 26-87; Ord. of 9-3-1991, art. IV, § 7)

417:
The Board of Commissioners or Planning Commission may at any time amend, modify, suspend or revoke any permit issued, upon cause, including, but not limited to, 1 or more of the following reasons:
(1) Violation of any condition or provision of such permit or failure to comply with any final order of the Board of Commissioners;
(2) Failure to comply with this article or any rule or regulation promulgated pursuant to this article;
(3) Obtaining a permit by misrepresentation or failure to disclose fully all relevant facts; or
(4) When the permitted activity poses a threat to the environment or to the health of humans.
(Code 2005, § 26-112; Ord. of 9-3-1991, art. V, § 3)

427:
(a) Unless otherwise prohibited by State or Federal law, the Board of Commissioners or Planning Commission may grant a variance from the requirements of this article or the rules and regulations effective hereunder if the Board of Commissioners or Planning Commission makes a finding that compliance with any provision of this article or any standard, rule or regulation:
(1) Would result in an arbitrary and unreasonable taking of property;
(2) Would result in effect in the closing and elimination of any lawful business, occupation or activity without sufficient corresponding benefit or advantage to the public;
(3) Would cause an unnecessary hardship;
(4) Would not substantially further the purposes of this article because of extraordinary and exceptional conditions pertaining to the particular piece of property in question because of its size, shape, or topography; or
(5) Would not substantially further the purposes of this article considering other relevant factors;
provided that no variance shall be granted where such variance would permit the continuation of a condition which poses an undue present or potential threat to the environment or to the health of humans; and, provided, further, that any variance so granted shall not be construed to relieve any person from any liability imposed by law, rule, order or regulation.
(b) Any person seeking a variance may file a request for a hearing in writing to the Planning Commission.
(c) The order of the Planning Commission shall be signed by the chair, shall state that it will be final unless an application for review is filed with the Chairperson within 10 days of the date of mailing, and shall be sent by certified mail, return receipt requested.
(d) Upon timely request by the applicant, the Board of Commissioners shall review the file, along with any recommendation of the Planning Commission, and shall render their decision. The order of the Board of Commissioners shall be signed and delivered to the applicant by certified mail, return receipt requested.
(Code 2005, § 26-171; Ord. of 9-3-1991, art. IX, § 1)

459:
The Chairperson may exercise the following duties and powers, in addition to the duties and powers conferred upon him elsewhere under this article, in order to fully implement this article:
(1) Advise, consult, cooperate and enter into contracts with any local, County, State or Federal official, and with any other person;
(2) Appoint committees;
(3) Appoint assistants and agents;
(4) Order appropriate testing, surveys and studies;
(5) Accept, receive, administer and disburse monies and grants; and
